{
  "title": "Common-source amplifier",
  "group": "Electronics — analog",
  "description": "common-source amplifier: gm·RD ≈ 5x inverting gain (β = 1 mA/V², Vov = 0.5 V, RD = 10 kΩ)",
  "schematic": {
    "notes": [
      {
        "x": 40,
        "y": -153,
        "title": "Common-source amplifier — what this testbench does",
        "lines": [
          "NMOS MN in common-source: VDD = 5 V through drain",
          "resistor RD = 10 kohm sets the operating point. The",
          "gate is biased at VB = 1.5 V with a small 50 mV,",
          "10 MHz sine VIN riding on top (Vov = 0.5 V).",
          "Small-signal gain is -gm*RD ~ -5, so probe vout at",
          "the drain is an inverted, ~5x copy of probe vin.",
          "Source is grounded (GS); CL is the output load.",
          "",
          "Set RD.R = 20000 and re-run to roughly double the",
          "gain (and watch the DC drain point drop)."
        ]
      }
    ],
